§ `B. "The index published last before" a day: the reading of month M-2 before the start of M, of M-1 before the end of M`

-- Assumption A1: a month's reading is published in the next month. Before 1 January 2026: November 2025 (published in December).
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2026 `month` 1 EQUALS 202511
-- Before 31 December 2025 (the end of December): November 2025, not December's own, which is published in January.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the end of` 2025 `month` 12 EQUALS 202511
-- Before the end of March 2026: the reading published in March itself, that of February.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the end of` 2026 `month` 3 EQUALS 202602
-- Before the start of March 2026: February's was published in March, not before it; January's was.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2026 `month` 3 EQUALS 202601
-- Across the year line: before the start of February 2026, December 2025.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2026 `month` 2 EQUALS 202512
-- The last month of the series: August 2026's reading is the last published before the end of September 2026.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the end of` 2026 `month` 9 EQUALS 202608
-- ... and before the start of October 2026.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2026 `month` 10 EQUALS 202608
-- One month later there is no reading: before the end of October 2026 would be September 2026's, not in the series (REFUSED by name).
#ASSERT REFUSED `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the end of` 2026 `month` 10
#ASSERT REFUSED `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2026 `month` 11
-- The first month of the series: January 2000's reading is the last published before the end of February 2000, and before the start of March.
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the end of` 2000 `month` 2 EQUALS 200001
#ASSERT `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2000 `month` 3 EQUALS 200001
-- Before the end of January 2000 would be December 1999's, and before the start of February 2000 as well: not in the series.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the end of` 2000 `month` 1
#ASSERT REFUSED `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2000 `month` 2
-- Before 1 January 2000: November 1999, not in the series.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the year-and-month of the reading last published before the start of` 2000 `month` 1
-- Reading the series directly: months outside it are refused by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the value of the reading for` 1999 `month` 12
#ASSERT REFUSED `the value of the reading for` 2026 `month` 9
#ASSERT REFUSED `the value of the reading for` 2030 `month` 1

§ `C. Restating a reading in an older base, against the values the linkage file prints`

-- Nov 2025 (103.6, base 2024) in base 2022: x 1.074 = 111.2664 (the linkage file prints 111.2664 for this row's 2022 column).
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 11)) `restated in base` 2022 EQUALS 1112664 / 10000
-- ... in base 2020: x 1.074 x 1.059 = 111.2664 x 1.059 = 117.8311176 (the file prints 117.8311).
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 11)) `restated in base` 2020 EQUALS 147288897 / 1250000
-- A reading in its own base is not changed.
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 11)) `restated in base` 2024 EQUALS 103.6
-- Nov 2024 (108.7, base 2022) in its own base and in base 2020 (x 1.059 = 115.1133, the file prints 115.1133).
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2024 `, number` 11)) `restated in base` 2022 EQUALS 108.7
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2024 `, number` 11)) `restated in base` 2020 EQUALS 1151133 / 10000
-- Aug 2026 (105.8) down through all twelve steps to base 1998: the file prints 170.156582039123; the product of the thirteen figures
-- 105.8 x 1.074 x 1.059 x 1.003 x 1.01 x 0.989 x 1.02 x 1.052 x 1.061 x 1.051 x 1.038 x 1.068 x 1.064 = 170.15658203912253... (the file's float, to its last digits).
#ASSERT (`the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2026 `, number` 8)) `restated in base` 1998) LESS THAN 170.1565820391226
#ASSERT (`the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2026 `, number` 8)) `restated in base` 1998) GREATER THAN 170.1565820391225
-- The step across the series' start: Jan 2001 (99.6, base 2000) in base 1998 = 99.6 x 1.064 = 105.9744 (the file prints 105.9744).
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2001 `, number` 1)) `restated in base` 1998 EQUALS 66234 / 625
-- The step across 2002 -> 2006 (base 2004 does not exist): Jan 2007 (99.0, base 2006) in base 2002 = 99.0 x 1.038 = 102.762.
#ASSERT `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2007 `, number` 1)) `restated in base` 2002 EQUALS 102.762
-- Restating into a NEWER base is not offered (REFUSED by name): Nov 2024 is base 2022; base 2024 is newer.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the value of` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2024 `, number` 11)) `restated in base` 2024

§ `D. The rise of the index in a tax year: within one base the Law's own arithmetic`

-- Tax year T's period runs 1 January T to 31 December T; its rise is November T against November T-1. s 120B(a) uses the rise in the
-- PREVIOUS tax year for the adjustment on 1 January of T+1. All pairs below are in one base.
-- Tax year 2024 (adjustment on 1 Jan 2025): Nov 2023 105.1 -> Nov 2024 108.7, both base 2022: 3.6 / 105.1 = 36 / 1051 (3.4%; the Bureau's 12-month figure for Nov 2024 is 3.4).
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2025 EQUALS 36 / 1051
-- Tax year 2022 (adjustment 1 Jan 2023): Nov 2021 102.3 -> Nov 2022 107.7, both base 2020: 5.4 / 102.3 = 54 / 1023 = 18 / 341.
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2023 EQUALS 18 / 341
-- Tax year 2020 (adjustment 1 Jan 2021): Nov 2019 100.8 -> Nov 2020 100.2, both base 2018: a FALL, -0.6 / 100.8 = -1 / 168. The signed rise is returned.
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2021 EQUALS 0 MINUS 1 / 168
-- Tax year 2006 (adjustment 1 Jan 2007): Nov 2005 103.2 -> Nov 2006 102.9, base 2002: a fall, -0.3 / 103.2 = -1 / 344.
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2007 EQUALS 0 MINUS 1 / 344
-- Tax year 2004 (adjustment 1 Jan 2005): Nov 2003 99.6 -> Nov 2004 100.5, base 2002: 0.9 / 99.6 = 3 / 332.
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2005 EQUALS 3 / 332
-- Tax year 2002 (adjustment 1 Jan 2003): Nov 2001 101.7 -> Nov 2002 108.5, base 2000: 6.8 / 101.7 = 68 / 1017.
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2003 EQUALS 68 / 1017
-- The same through the pair of readings that row IL-03 takes as an input: (Nov 2023, Nov 2024) = (105.1, 108.7), then s 1's arithmetic on them.
#ASSERT (`s 120B(a) — the readings for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2025)'s `index last published before the start of the period` EQUALS 105.1
#ASSERT (`s 120B(a) — the readings for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2025)'s `index last published before the end of the period` EQUALS 108.7
-- A same-base answer does not depend on the switch: all three readings give the same.
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2025 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 36 / 1051
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2025 `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct EQUALS 36 / 1051
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2025 `, comparing readings of two bases as` decl EQUALS 36 / 1051
-- Boundary months. The earliest tax year whose rise exists is 2001 (Nov 2000 against Nov 1999 would need 1999): tax year 2000 is REFUSED, by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2001
-- The latest: tax year 2026 would need November 2026: REFUSED, by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2027
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2028 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link

§ `E. s 120B(b): the rise from the start of the tax year to the end of a month`

-- Tax year 2026, the start reading is Nov 2025 (103.6, base 2024); the end reading is the reading of the month before the named one (published in it).
-- end of January: Dec 2025 103.6: 0 / 103.6 = 0.
#ASSERT `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 1 EQUALS 0
-- end of February: Jan 2026 103.3: -0.3 / 103.6 = -3 / 1036.
#ASSERT `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 2 EQUALS 0 MINUS 3 / 1036
-- end of March: Feb 2026 103.5: -0.1 / 103.6 = -1 / 1036.
#ASSERT `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 3 EQUALS 0 MINUS 1 / 1036
-- end of April: Mar 2026 103.9: 0.3 / 103.6 = 3 / 1036.
#ASSERT `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 4 EQUALS 3 / 1036
-- end of September: Aug 2026 105.8 (the last reading): 2.2 / 103.6 = 22 / 1036 = 11 / 518.
#ASSERT `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 9 EQUALS 11 / 518
-- end of October needs Sep 2026: REFUSED, by name. A month that is not 1 to 12 is REFUSED, by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 10
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 13
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2026 `from its start to the end of month` 0
-- Tax year 2001, end of January: the start reading is Nov 2000 (106.7, base 1998); the end reading is Dec 2000 (106.6, base 1998): -0.1 / 106.7 = -1 / 1067.
#ASSERT `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2001 `from its start to the end of month` 1 EQUALS 0 MINUS 1 / 1067
-- Tax year 2000, end of February: the start reading would be Nov 1999: REFUSED, by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(b) — the rate of rise of the index in tax year` 2000 `from its start to the end of month` 2

§ `F. Across a change of base: the Bureau re-based; the text does not say how readings of two bases are compared (fork F1)`

-- The case row IL-04 needs: s 334(a)(1) for 1 January 2026 = Nov 2025 (103.6, base 2024) against Nov 2024 (108.7, base 2022).
--   L: 103.6 x 1.074 = 111.2664 in base 2022;  (111.2664 - 108.7) / 108.7 = 2.5664 / 108.7 = 25664 / 1087000 = 3208 / 135875  (2.36%).
--   P: the Bureau's 12-month percentage for Nov 2025 is 2.4, so 2.4 / 100 = 24 / 1000 = 3 / 125.
--   They differ, so the default DECLINES (REFUSED by name); each reading is kept and gives its own number.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2026
#ASSERT `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2026 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 3208 / 135875
#ASSERT `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2026 `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct EQUALS 3 / 125
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2026 `, comparing readings of two bases as` decl
-- The same pair through s 120B(a): the rise in tax year 2025 (adjustment on 1 Jan 2026) is the same two readings, so the same numbers.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2026
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2026 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 3208 / 135875
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2026 `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct EQUALS 3 / 125
-- The pair on one scale, at L: (108.7, 111.2664) in base 2022; at the default it is declined, as the rise is.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the readings for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2026
#ASSERT (`the readings on one scale for` (`the period of tax year` 2025) `, comparing readings of two bases as` link)'s `index last published before the start of the period` EQUALS 108.7
#ASSERT (`the readings on one scale for` (`the period of tax year` 2025) `, comparing readings of two bases as` link)'s `index last published before the end of the period` EQUALS 1112664 / 10000
-- The pair cannot be given at P: the Bureau's percentage is a rate, not a pair of readings.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the readings on one scale for` (`the period of tax year` 2025) `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct
-- Tax year 2023 (adjustment 1 Jan 2024): Nov 2022 (107.7, base 2020) -> Nov 2023 (105.1, base 2022).
--   L: 105.1 x 1.059 = 111.3009 in base 2020;  (111.3009 - 107.7) / 107.7 = 3.6009 / 107.7 = 36009 / 1077000 = 12003 / 359000.
--   P: Nov 2023's 12-month percentage is 3.3, so 33 / 1000.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2024
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2024 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 12003 / 359000
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2024 `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct EQUALS 33 / 1000
-- Tax year 2001 (adjustment 1 Jan 2002): Nov 2000 (106.7, base 1998) -> Nov 2001 (101.7, base 2000): the earliest base change in the series.
--   L: 101.7 x 1.064 = 108.2088 in base 1998;  (108.2088 - 106.7) / 106.7 = 1.5088 / 106.7 = 15088 / 1067000 = 1886 / 133375.
--   P: Nov 2001's 12-month percentage is 1.4, so 14 / 1000 = 7 / 500.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2002
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2002 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 1886 / 133375
#ASSERT `s 120B(a) — the rate of rise of the index for the adjustment on 1 January of tax year` 2002 `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct EQUALS 7 / 500
-- One month across a change of base: Dec 2000 (106.6, base 1998) -> Jan 2001 (99.6, base 2000), two readings taken directly.
--   L: 99.6 x 1.064 = 105.9744;  (105.9744 - 106.6) / 106.6 = -0.6256 / 106.6 = -6256 / 1066000 = -391 / 66625  (-0.59%; the Bureau prints -0.6).
--   P: a one-month span is not twelve months: REFUSED by name. The default: REFUSED.
#ASSERT `the rate of rise of the index from` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2000 `, number` 12)) `to` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2001 `, number` 1)) `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 0 MINUS 391 / 66625
#ASSERT REFUSED `the rate of rise of the index from` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2000 `, number` 12)) `to` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2001 `, number` 1)) `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct
#ASSERT REFUSED `the rate of rise of the index from` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2000 `, number` 12)) `to` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2001 `, number` 1))
-- Three years across two changes of base: Nov 2022 (107.7, base 2020) -> Nov 2025 (103.6, base 2024), restated in base 2020.
--   L: 103.6 x 1.074 x 1.059 = 117.8311176;  (117.8311176 - 107.7) / 107.7 = 10.1311176 / 107.7 = 4221299 / 44875000.
--   P: three years is not twelve months: REFUSED by name.
#ASSERT `the rate of rise of the index from` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2022 `, number` 11)) `to` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 11)) `, comparing readings of two bases as` link EQUALS 4221299 / 44875000
#ASSERT REFUSED `the rate of rise of the index from` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2022 `, number` 11)) `to` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 11)) `, comparing readings of two bases as` pct
#ASSERT REFUSED `the rate of rise of the index from` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2022 `, number` 11)) `to` (`the reading the Bureau published for` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 11))
-- WHERE THE READINGS AGREE the default answers. Synthetic readings (NOT the Bureau's): November 2024 = 100.0 in base 2022 and November 2025 = 100.0
-- in base 2024 with the Bureau's twelve-month percentage 7.4. L: 100.0 x 1.074 = 107.4; (107.4 - 100) / 100 = 0.074 = 37 / 500. P: 7.4 / 100 = 37 / 500. They agree.
#ASSERT `the rate of rise of the index from` (`A published reading` WITH `the year the reading is of` IS 2024, `the month the reading is of` IS 11, `the base year of the reading` IS 2022, `the value of the reading` IS 100, `the percent the Bureau published for the month` IS 0, `the percent the Bureau published for the twelve months` IS 0) `to` (`A published reading` WITH `the year the reading is of` IS 2025, `the month the reading is of` IS 11, `the base year of the reading` IS 2024, `the value of the reading` IS 100, `the percent the Bureau published for the month` IS 0, `the percent the Bureau published for the twelve months` IS 7.4) EQUALS 37 / 500

§ `G. National Insurance Law s 334(a)(1): the years it applies to`

-- It applies on 1 January 2026, 2027 and 2028 only. 2025 and 2029 are REFUSED by name (2029 on uses the average wage).
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2025
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2029
-- 2027 and 2028 pass the gate but need Nov 2026 and Nov 2027, not in the series: REFUSED, by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2027 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the rate of rise of the index for 1 January of` 2028 `, comparing readings of two bases as` link
-- The pair for 2026 is declined at the default, as the rise is; for 2025 and 2029 declined by the gate.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the readings for 1 January of` 2026
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 334(a)(1) — the readings for 1 January of` 2025

§ `H. s 120B(e)(2): the rise on 1 January 2028 (fork F2): the periods the readings name`

-- The default declines; the two readings are kept by name and name their periods.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(e)(2) — the period of the rise on 1 January 2028, reading` `section 120B(e)(2) — the reading this row takes of the rise on 1 January 2028`
GIVEN k IS A `A reading of the rise s 120B(e)(2) takes on 1 January 2028`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the first month of the period s 120B(e)(2) takes, as year-and-month, at` k MEANS
    (p's `the first month of the period`)'s `year of the month` TIMES 100 PLUS (p's `the first month of the period`)'s `number of the month in the year`
    WHERE p MEANS `s 120B(e)(2) — the period of the rise on 1 January 2028, reading` k

GIVEN k IS A `A reading of the rise s 120B(e)(2) takes on 1 January 2028`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the last month of the period s 120B(e)(2) takes, as year-and-month, at` k MEANS
    (p's `the last month of the period`)'s `year of the month` TIMES 100 PLUS (p's `the last month of the period`)'s `number of the month in the year`
    WHERE p MEANS `s 120B(e)(2) — the period of the rise on 1 January 2028, reading` k

-- 2027 alone: 1 January 2027 to 31 December 2027.
#ASSERT `the first month of the period s 120B(e)(2) takes, as year-and-month, at` `the rise of the index in tax year 2027 alone` EQUALS 202701
#ASSERT `the last month of the period s 120B(e)(2) takes, as year-and-month, at` `the rise of the index in tax year 2027 alone` EQUALS 202712
-- From the start of 2024 to the end of 2027: the amounts are those of 1 January 2024, so the rise of tax years 2024 to 2027 is the one to catch up.
#ASSERT `the first month of the period s 120B(e)(2) takes, as year-and-month, at` `the rise of the index from the start of tax year 2024 to the end of tax year 2027` EQUALS 202401
#ASSERT `the last month of the period s 120B(e)(2) takes, as year-and-month, at` `the rise of the index from the start of tax year 2024 to the end of tax year 2027` EQUALS 202712
-- Neither rise can be computed from the sources: November 2027 (and November 2026) are later than the series (REFUSED by name); the default is declined for the fork.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(e)(2) — the rate of rise of the index on 1 January 2028`
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(e)(2) — the rate of rise of the index on 1 January 2028, reading` `the rise of the index in tax year 2027 alone` `, comparing readings of two bases as` link
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 120B(e)(2) — the rate of rise of the index on 1 January 2028, reading` `the rise of the index from the start of tax year 2024 to the end of tax year 2027` `, comparing readings of two bases as` link

§ `I. A twelve-month period that is not a tax year`

-- The period of twelve months beginning with March 2025 runs to the end of February 2026: its last month is February 2026.
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the last month of the twelve months beginning with` y `month` m `, as year-and-month` MEANS
    (p's `the last month of the period`)'s `year of the month` TIMES 100 PLUS (p's `the last month of the period`)'s `number of the month in the year`
    WHERE p MEANS `the period of twelve months beginning with` (`the calendar month in year` y `, number` m)

#ASSERT `the last month of the twelve months beginning with` 2025 `month` 3 `, as year-and-month` EQUALS 202602
#ASSERT `the last month of the twelve months beginning with` 2024 `month` 1 `, as year-and-month` EQUALS 202412
#ASSERT `the last month of the twelve months beginning with` 2025 `month` 12 `, as year-and-month` EQUALS 202611
-- Its rise: the reading published last before the start of March 2025 is January 2025's (101.5, base 2024); the one published last before the end
-- of February 2026 is January 2026's (103.3, base 2024): (103.3 - 101.5) / 101.5 = 1.8 / 101.5 = 18 / 1015. One base, so every reading of the switch agrees.
#ASSERT `the rate of rise of the index in` (`the period of twelve months beginning with` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 3)) EQUALS 18 / 1015
-- The twelve months beginning with January 2024 are the tax year 2024: the same 36 / 1051 as section D.
#ASSERT `the rate of rise of the index in` (`the period of twelve months beginning with` (`the calendar month in year` 2024 `, number` 1)) EQUALS 36 / 1051
-- The twelve months beginning with December 2025 end with November 2026: not in the series, REFUSED by name.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the rate of rise of the index in` (`the period of twelve months beginning with` (`the calendar month in year` 2025 `, number` 12))

§ `J. EVIDENCE ONLY, not law: which reading of fork F1 the figure printed in the National Insurance Law reproduces`

-- NII s 334(a), the definition of the reduced collection threshold (source line 3605), prints 7,522 shekels and, in an editorial note of the consolidation (an aid), the 2026 figure 7,703 shekels.
-- The text states no rounding. 7,522 x (1 + 3 / 125) [reading P] = 7,522 x 1.024 = 7,702.528 (nearest whole shekel 7,703: the printed figure).
-- 7,522 x (1 + 3208 / 135875) [reading L] = 7,522 x 139083 / 135875 = 7,699.6... (nearest whole shekel 7,700: not the printed figure).
-- This does not decide the fork (the Institute's method is not in the sources), and the default stays declined; it is the reason the fork is not a technicality.
#ASSERT 7522 TIMES (1 + 3 / 125) EQUALS 7702.528
#ASSERT 7522 TIMES (1 + 3208 / 135875) GREATER THAN 7699.5
#ASSERT 7522 TIMES (1 + 3208 / 135875) LESS THAN 7700
